{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts n-digit positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    It uses the inclusion-exclusion principle and handles edge cases for n=1 and n=2.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Count numbers starting with 1\n    count_starts = 1 if n == 1 else pow(10, n - 1)\n\n    # Count numbers ending with 1\n    count_ends = 1 if n == 1 else 9 * pow(10, n - 2)\n\n    # Count numbers both starting and ending with 1\n    count_both = 1 if n <= 2 else pow(10, n - 2)\n\n    # Apply inclusion-exclusion principle\n    total_count = count_starts + count_ends - count_both\n\n    return total_count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/83", "solution": "Looking at the generated code, I notice it's in C++ format rather than Python. Let me generate the correct Python function based on the design:\n\n```python\ndef starts_one_ends(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return the count of the numbers of n-digit\n    positive integers that start or end with 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle edge case for n=1\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    # Count numbers starting with 1: 10^(n-1)\n    starting_with_1 = 10 ** (n - 1)\n\n    # Count numbers ending with 1: 9 * 10^(n-2)\n    # (first digit can be 1-9, last digit must be 1, middle digits can be 0-9)\n    ending_with_1 = 9 * (10 ** (n - 2))\n\n    # Count numbers both starting and ending with 1: 10^(n-2)\n    # (first and last digits are 1, middle digits can be 0-9)\n    both_start_end_with_1 = 10 ** (n - 2)\n\n    # Apply inclusion-exclusion principle to avoid double counting\n    return starting_with_1 + ending_with_1 - both_start_end_with_1\n```"}
